@@ -0,0 +1,27 @@
+---
+id: dl-002-json-patch-application-semantics-for-applypatch
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Patch application semantics for applyPatch"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Services need to update JSON documents with standard patches. RFC 6902 leaves some behaviour open
+(atomicity, input mutation, equality, error kinds).
+
+## Decision
+
+- The input document is deep-cloned (`structuredClone`) and operations run on the clone; values taken from the patch are cloned too. No input is mutated, and a failing patch returns nothing (atomic).
+- Patch must be an array; each operation an object with a known `op` and a string `path`; `value` is required (own member, `null` allowed) for add/replace/test; `from` is a string for move/copy. Unknown members are ignored (RFC 4).
+- Pointers follow `resolvePointer` (RFC 6901, string form). `-` is valid only as the last token of `add`; array add indexes are `0..length`.
+- remove, replace, move source and test require the location to exist; remove of the root throws; add/replace on the root replace the document.
+- `move` onto its own path is a no-op (source must exist); into its own child throws.
+- `test` uses JSON equality: object key order ignored, array order significant, strict scalar types.
+- Members are written with `defineProperty` so `__proto__` is an ordinary key and the prototype is never touched.
+- All failures throw `Error`; a non-string pointer throws `TypeError`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Callers get all-or-nothing updates and may reuse inputs freely, at the cost of a full clone per call.
+Documents must be structured-cloneable JSON (functions throw).
